<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Harness.RigRead</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>rig-read</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THIS BUILD READS AND NOTHING ELSE.

    It is a SEPARATE executable from rig-write on purpose. rig-write plans a governed write and owns
    no socket at all (a property its tests assert); this one owns a socket and owns no write. Neither
    project can quietly acquire the other's half, because the halves are not in the same assembly.

    What it may reach: Sharp7Client (the real IS7Client) for ConnectTo/DBRead/GetOrderCode/PlcGetStatus,
    and Sharp7 directly for MBRead — marker memory, which IS7Client does not expose. As of 2026-08-14
    that is TWO callers, not one: the fallback diagnostic that tells a CPU-wide refusal apart from a
    block-specific one, and the 'marker' flag, a first-class read of the harness mirror (%M, not
    in a DB, so no DB read can reach it). Both are reads. Nothing here calls MBWrite, DBWrite,
    WriteArea, WriteBit, PlcStop, Download or Delete; the last four are not even on IS7Client.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.S7\Harness.S7.csproj" />

    <!--
      For MarkerDbLayout, and only that. The layout of the marker DB is ONE fact and both the write
      planner and this reader must address it identically — a second copy of those four offsets is
      exactly the drift that makes a read "confirm" numbers a write never used. Referencing the
      write-planner assembly grants nothing, because it owns no socket and no Sharp7 reference; the
      dependency runs reader -> planner, never planner -> reader.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.RigWrite\Harness.RigWrite.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Sharp7 comes from NuGet as of 2026-09-20; the decision and its 🔴 binary-swap caveat are in
    Directory.Packages.props. The ErrorMissingSharp7 target that used to live here is gone with it.
    THAT TARGET WAS THE REASON THIS PROJECT WAS ONE OF THE TWO RECORDED cannotBuild TARGETS, and it
    was right to exist: a client that cannot open a socket is not a reduced version of this tool, it
    is nothing, so failing with a named reason beat failing with a list of unresolved type names.
    With the dependency restorable on any machine there is no absence left for it to explain.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Sharp7" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
